WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.618
Welcome to Module Four: Fine-Tuning.

2
00:00:02.948 --> 00:00:06.717
You'll learn when to fine-tune, and how to do it well on one GPU.

3
00:00:07.517 --> 00:00:11.435
Picture an insurance company whose support assistant keeps going off script.

4
00:00:11.765 --> 00:00:15.277
It rambles, ignores the house tone, and breaks the ticket format.

5
00:00:16.077 --> 00:00:20.187
The team's first instinct is to fine-tune it on every policy document.

6
00:00:20.517 --> 00:00:24.158
But policies change weekly, so the model would be stale by Monday.

7
00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:29.175
Lesson one sorts the levers, from prompting and RAG to full fine-tuning.

8
00:00:29.505 --> 00:00:34.233
Missing knowledge needs retrieval; behaviour, format, and style need fine-tuning.

9
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.503
So the team keeps RAG for facts, and fine-tunes for behaviour.

10
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:44.629
Lesson two's lab runs supervised fine-tuning of a small Qwen model with TRL.

11
00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.197
You'll train only on the assistant's replies, with the right chat template.

12
00:00:49.527 --> 00:00:53.125
Then you'll compare outputs before and after, and read the loss curves.

13
00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:56.121
But fully fine-tuning a seven billion

14
00:00:56.171 --> 00:00:58.974
parameter model needs over a hundred gigabytes.

15
00:00:59.304 --> 00:01:00.683
That's where LoRA comes in.

16
00:01:01.483 --> 00:01:05.871
LoRA freezes the base model and trains two small matrices beside each layer.

17
00:01:06.201 --> 00:01:10.460
Only a tiny fraction of the weights learn, and the adapter is just megabytes.

18
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.178
QLoRA goes further, loading the frozen base in four-bit precision.

19
00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:20.898
In the lab, you'll QLoRA-tune Qwen on a free Colab T4, then merge the adapter.

20
00:01:21.698 --> 00:01:25.723
Now the format is right, but reviewers still prefer some answers over others.

21
00:01:26.053 --> 00:01:29.224
Lesson four turns those preferences into a training signal.

22
00:01:30.024 --> 00:01:34.774
Classic RLHF trains a reward model, then optimizes with PPO.

23
00:01:35.104 --> 00:01:40.046
DPO skips the reward model and learns straight from chosen and rejected pairs.

24
00:01:40.846 --> 00:01:44.849
GRPO scores a group of sampled answers against each other.

25
00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:48.863
With checkable rewards it's powerful, but watch for reward hacking.

26
00:01:49.663 --> 00:01:53.261
Lesson five maps it all to NVIDIA NeMo, at cluster scale.

27
00:01:53.591 --> 00:01:58.426
You'll compare p-tuning, adapters, IA3, and LoRA by where they add parameters.

28
00:01:59.226 --> 00:02:03.869
You'll also guard against catastrophic forgetting, where new skills erase old ones.

29
00:02:04.199 --> 00:02:08.843
Lower learning rates, fewer epochs, and mixed-in general data protect old skills.

30
00:02:09.643 --> 00:02:12.174
Fine-tuning is thirteen percent of the exam.

31
00:02:12.504 --> 00:02:17.425
Expect method choice, LoRA memory math, and DPO versus RLHF.

32
00:02:18.225 --> 00:02:23.209
By the end, you'll fine-tune, align, and ship a small model on a single GPU.

33
00:02:23.539 --> 00:02:26.753
Let's begin with lesson one: choosing an adaptation method.
